  "summary": "The health-tech sector is navigating significant shifts across funding, policy, and clinical integration. Tiny Health secured $33 million in Series B funding to expand microbiome testing, while EliseAI raised $350 million at a $4 billion valuation to scale healthcare AI automation, demonstrating continued venture appetite despite broader economic concerns. On the pharma side, AstraZeneca invested $2 billion in Summit Therapeutics for cancer drug development, and Novo agreed to pay up to $2.6 billion to license a weekly weight loss pill from China's Hengrui Pharma, signaling continued M&A and licensing activity in high-value therapeutic areas. Policy uncertainty looms ahead of the midterm elections, with lawmakers urging the Trump administration to reconsider restrictions on imported medications from Canada and overseas, while questions persist around Medicare's elimination of automatic breakthrough device designations and potential shifts in drug pricing, Medicaid, and research funding. In parallel, healthcare organizations are addressing practical challenges including clinician-driven data leakage, shadow AI risks on devices, and communication breakdowns, while the MAHA movement's evolution toward corporate participation is drawing scrutiny from grassroots supporters.",
